That one FAIL was real: the address and route that left and returned with
the adapter were recorded, but the `link.down`/`link.up` pair for the
disable/enable itself was not. Section 3 is the investigation.

## 3. A missed link event, found by this run and fixed

Three disable/enable rounds against the installed service, reading the
link collector's debug log for ifIndex 16 alongside the record:

    [10:55:11.692] round 1: disable
    [10:55:19.161] round 1: enable
    [10:55:28.241] round 2: disable
    [10:55:34.825] round 2: enable
    [10:55:43.956] round 3: disable
    [10:55:50.598] round 3: enable
    --- link events ---
    TIME          KIND       SUBJECT     SEV     DETAIL
    10:55:28.336  link.down  Ethernet 2  warn    x2 admin_up=false cause=administrative
    10:55:34.897  link.up    Ethernet 2  notice  x2 admin_up=true down_duration_ms=6561
    --- link collector lines for ifindex 16 ---
    2026-09-14T10:55:12.635+04:00 ifname="Ethernet 2" ifindex=16 kind=0 admin_up=true oper_up=true oper_status=1 media=1 mtu=1500 seen=true
    2026-09-14T10:55:12.636+04:00 ifname="Ethernet 2" ifindex=16 kind=2 admin_up=true oper_up=true oper_status=1 media=1 mtu=1500 seen=true
    ...
    2026-09-14T10:55:28.336+04:00 ifname="Ethernet 2" ifindex=16 kind=0 admin_up=false oper_up=false oper_status=2 media=0 mtu=1500 seen=true
    ...
    2026-09-14T10:55:44.036+04:00 ifname="Ethernet 2" ifindex=16 kind=0 admin_up=true oper_up=true oper_status=1 media=1 mtu=1500 seen=true
    2026-09-14T10:55:50.683+04:00 ifname="Ethernet 2" ifindex=16 kind=1 admin_up=true oper_up=false oper_status=5 media=1 mtu=1500 seen=true

One round in three produced the pair (the `x2` is the store's own
de-duplication of the two notifications a single change delivers, one per
address family - log 18). In the other two rounds every notification for
the disable arrived while `GetIpInterfaceEntry` still returned the
*previous* state (`admin_up=true oper_up=true` at 10:55:12 and 10:55:44,
milliseconds after the `Disable-NetAdapter` call): the callback fires
before the interface table reflects the change, so re-reading the row on
notification alone can see nothing to report. The enable side has the
mirror image: `oper_status=5` (dormant) at first, `up` a few milliseconds
later.

Fix (`internal/collect/iphelper/link_windows.go`): every notification
also schedules two delayed re-reads of the same row (500 ms and 2.5 s
later), so a change whose row lags the callback is still observed once the
table catches up; the analyzer only emits on an actual state difference,
so a re-read that finds nothing new emits nothing. A related defect had
been fixed earlier the same morning, before the runs in sections 1 and 2:
an interface the collector had already reported was being dropped by the
interface-row filter while down (no media, MTU 0), so even a correctly
read `down` row could be filtered out - a tracked interface now bypasses
the observability filter.

Five disable/enable rounds against the rebuilt service, all recorded:

    TIME          KIND       SUBJECT     SEV     DETAIL
    10:57:20.911  link.down  Ethernet 2  warn    x5 admin_up=false cause=administrative
    10:57:26.542  link.up    Ethernet 2  notice  x5 admin_up=true down_duration_ms=5616

